<?xml version="1.0" encoding="ISO-8859-1"?>

<!--
  xscreensaver hack config descriptor. Registers "Neo Pipes" in
  xscreensaver-demo's hack list and its settings dialog. Named
  "pipes-xscreensaver" (matching the binary), not "pipes" - the real
  xscreensaver package already ships its own GL-based hack literally
  called "pipes", and this must not collide with it.

  No <number>/<boolean>/<select> controls: unlike most hacks, this one
  has no per-run CLI tunables of its own. Every setting (pipe count,
  speed, style, palette, camera) lives in the shared AppConfig file, and
  is edited via the standalone "Pipes Settings" app installed alongside
  this package - not through this dialog. Real-world XML structure
  (attribute names, <command>/<_description> shape) confirmed against
  xscreensaver's own upstream hacks/config/*.xml files (e.g. pipes.xml,
  hypercube.xml). The driver's actual invocation has now been verified
  against a live xscreensaver 6.08 too: the daemon passes no arguments
  of its own and hands the hack its window through the
  XSCREENSAVER_WINDOW environment variable instead. The -root below is
  kept because it is the convention every stock hack's config carries,
  and it is correctly read as "no specific window was named" - the
  environment variable outranks it, which is what keeps the hack drawing
  inside xscreensaver's window rather than behind it. See
  crates/pipes-xscreensaver/src/args.rs and docs/ROADMAP.md.
-->
<screensaver name="pipes-xscreensaver" _label="Neo Pipes">

  <command arg="-root"/>

  <_description>
A cross-platform recreation of the classic Windows 3D Pipes screensaver:
colored pipes grow through a 3D grid, turning at random joints, until the
scene fills up and resets.

All settings (pipe count, speed, style, palette, camera) are configured
via the standalone "Pipes Settings" app installed alongside this
package, not via this dialog.

https://github.com/brando5393/neo_win_pipes
  </_description>
</screensaver>
